From 7fc9de50c402b971f0e3f285fe18f9ae119c5af1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 11:10:24 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] feat(4leaf): original D6 dice and battle score numerals
 from OBS.pak

Two OBS members filed as chessman cards are the game's dice UI:
- 1171.obs (card 0171): frames 0-5 are D6 result faces 1..6 (46x49),
  frames 6+ a white die tumbling in 3D (60x71). Same art as the dice in
  the archived 800x600 battle.jpg. Face = pips-1, VERIFIED by pip count.
- 1167.obs (card 0167): frames 0-7 are battle.jpg's white, red-outlined
  score digits 0..7.

Runtime
- Falling D6 (dice-fall.js): plays original tumble frames 6,7,10,18,25 in
  flight and lands on the original result face. Drop/bounce path is still
  the WEB reconstruction; the CSS cube stays only as a load-failure
  fallback. D4 keeps the WEB tetra (no original D4 art found).
- Battle dice: original result faces, 46x49 (23x25 on phones), no rotation.
- Battle score: original numerals when both digits are 0..7; scores with
  8 or 9 keep WEB text because those frames are not extracted.
- Battle overlay (pointer-events:none) now paints above the WEB top-left
  minimap, which clipped the larger original numerals. battle.jpg has no
  minimap there.

Colour-key bug
- The decoder treats palette index 0 as transparent. In VIDEO-RLE only
  skipped pixels are transparent: the "1" pip of 1171 is index 0 and is
  opaque pink-red in battle.jpg. decode_rows(alpha_from_runs=True) adds the
  correct rule (default unchanged; synthetic test added). Other OBS frames
  may have the same holes and need re-extraction.
- tools/correct-obs-colorkey-holes.py restores enclosed holes whose RGB is
  the frame's palette[0] for the 11 dice frames only (provenance.json has
  source/corrected SHA-256; 280 pixels restored, 0 invented).

PWA caches the corrected frames.

